Joint Dislocation
Acute Urgent Care
Gross joint deformity, Inability to move joint, Palpable empty joint space, Loss of articular congruity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-037
Reason for Visit: Extreme pain and obvious deformity of the shoulder following a fall.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who tripped and fell onto an outstretched arm, resulting in a visibly deformed shoulder and agonizing pain. Urgent face-to-face assessment was necessary to provide immediate, potent analgesia, perform a critical neurovascular exam to rule out arterial/nerve compression, and facilitate safe transport for reduction, as prolonged dislocation causes irreversible cartilage damage and avascular necrosis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My shoulder popped completely out of its socket when I fell, and my arm is stuck out like this and killing me!
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a severe injury to the right shoulder that occurred 45 minutes ago after tripping on a rug and falling onto an outstretched hand. The patient felt an immediate, sickening "pop" followed by severe agonizing pain localized strictly to the glenohumeral articulation. There is an absolute inability to actively or passively move the joint. The patient notes an abnormal prominence at the front of the shoulder. The patient describes a slight numbness/tingling radiating down the outer aspect of the upper arm, suggesting early axillary nerve stretching.
Functional Status: Sitting on a dining chair, supporting the right elbow with the left hand, leaning slightly to the right.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe pain; unable to safely mobilize to a vehicle without strong analgesia and splinting.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is in obvious severe distress, sweaty, and holding the right arm in slight abduction and external rotation.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals an obvious gross structural deformity at the right shoulder joint line, with a distinctive "squared-off" appearance compared to the contralateral unaffected joint. Palpation reveals an empty joint space beneath the acromion, and an abnormal prominence of the displaced bony articular head palpable anteriorly. There is localized swelling but no open wounds. A neurovascular exam reveals an intact radial pulse and capillary refill <2 seconds. However, there is decreased sensation over the lateral deltoid (axillary nerve distribution). Expected investigations include plain radiographs confirming a complete loss of articular congruity with anterior displacement of the humeral head.
Environmental Observations: Tripped over a loose rug; hazard identified and moved by roommate.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. No analgesia taken prior to arrival.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Joint Dislocation - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Clavicular Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to the deformity being located specifically at the glenohumeral joint with an empty socket, rather than a step-off deformity mid-shaft along the collarbone.

Proximal Humerus Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely as a solitary injury due to the palpable empty glenoid fossa, though a concomitant fracture-dislocation must be excluded via X-ray prior to reduction.

Acromioclavicular (AC) Joint Separation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the arm being locked in abduction/external rotation and the empty glenoid, whereas an AC separation presents with a prominent distal clavicle but an intact shoulder socket.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic diagnostic tetrad of an anterior shoulder dislocation: gross joint deformity, an absolute inability to move the joint, a palpable empty joint space beneath the acromion, and a high likelihood of loss of articular congruity on X-ray. The presence of axillary nerve neuropraxia makes this a time-sensitive orthopedic urgency. An after-hours home visit was justified to provide immediate parenteral analgesia to break the severe muscle spasm and to arrange safe transport to the Emergency Department, as attempting to move the patient without proper splinting and pain relief would cause intolerable agony and potentially worsen the nerve injury.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg and Subcutaneous Morphine 5mg for synergistic, potent analgesia. Applied a broad arm sling to support the weight of the limb and minimize traction on the joint capsule. Advised the roommate to drive the patient immediately to the nearest Emergency Department for X-ray and procedural sedation/reduction.
Positive Behavioral Change: By following the physiotherapist's strict rehabilitation exercises for the next 6 weeks, you will rebuild the torn rotator cuff muscles, which is the only way to prevent your shoulder from easily popping out again in the future.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Transport via private vehicle to ED.
DME/Safety (Optional): Broad arm sling provided.
Follow-up: Orthopedic/Physiotherapy review after hospital reduction.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the hand suddenly turns completely pale, cold, or completely numb, pull over and call 000 immediately, as this indicates the displaced bone has pinched off the main artery or nerve bundle to your arm.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The shoulder is clearly out of joint, but the hospital must take an X-ray first to ensure there are no small bone fractures before they attempt to pull it back into place.
Clinical Course: Under deep sedation at the hospital, the doctors will gently manipulate the arm back into the socket, which usually provides instantaneous pain relief.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not let anyone try to "pop it back in" outside of the hospital. Attempting this without an X-ray and proper muscle relaxants can shatter the humerus bone or permanently sever the axillary nerve.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I have given you a strong combination of painkillers to stop the severe muscle spasms that are currently locking the bone in the wrong position. The sling will support the weight of your arm to make the car ride bearable.
Actionable Steps: Have your roommate drive smoothly. Keep your arm resting heavily in the sling. Do not eat or drink anything, as you will need strong fasting sedation medication at the hospital for the relocation procedure.

